only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina sits just right of the country overall, with a partisan lean of about one point Republican, making it one of the closest presidential battlegrounds. With the incumbent term-limited, both parties will nominate new candidates, removing any incumbency advantage and leaving the state's underlying lean as the anchor.\n\nNo general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available yet, so the state's fundamentals carry the call. That makes this effectively a tossup with a small Republican edge. Uncertainty is wide, since the nominees and the national climate are still taking shape, and headline coverage so far focuses on the 2028 primaries rather than the fall contest. Early general-election polling once the nominations settle will do the most to clarify which way the state tips.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}]}